# Rule 4 of the product direction (issue #3): focus and order are sacred. The window you switch to comes to the front and
# into view, and is never left behind another.
# Story: somebody lives framed on one window and switches with ⌘Tab, the Dock and the Window menu, which all reach Arbor
# as "this window has the keyboard now". This is the half that needs no Mac; that macOS's reports arrive is board 1.2.
quiet
open Safari
open Terminal
open Mail
open Notes 400x300
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
dock 103 beside 102 bottom
move 104 560 300 400 300
zoom 101
expect fills 101
expect in-front 101
expect hidden 102
# Notes floats: it is where it was, behind Safari.
expect still 104
expect visible 104
expect at 104 560 300 400x300
# To a docked window out of view: framed stays framed, and Back returns.
focus 102
expect fills 102
expect focused 102
expect in-front 102
expect hidden 101
back
expect fills 101
# To a floating window: it is in view already, behind the framed one. It comes to the front where it is, and no view moves.
focus 104
expect still
expect camera 101
expect fills 101
expect visible 104
expect focused 104
expect in-front 104
expect at 104 560 300 400x300
# From a group the view steps out just far enough.
focus 102
out
expect visible 103
expect hidden 101
focus 101
expect visible 101
expect visible 102
expect focused 101
expect in-front 101
# To a window in view: nothing moves.
focus 103
expect still
expect focused 103
expect in-front 103
# Out of the Dock, and out of ⌘H.
zoom 101
minimize 102
restore 102
expect visible 102
expect focused 102
expect in-front 102
hide Mail
unhide Mail
focus 103
expect visible 103
expect in-front 103
# An app that only reports a window of its own, in the background, moves no view.
zoom 101
background-focus 103
wait 2
expect fills 101
expect focused 101
